seg-022859135e79 = <b>A sixth paper in the Research table.</b> "Cislunar navigation is an observability problem: arc-length thresholds, Gramian conditioning, and the Doppler lever" (<a1>arXiv:{ "2610.23871" }</a1>, eess.SP) shows that dilution of precision is undefined for range-only cislunar tracking and uses the observability Gramian on four planar distant retrograde orbits instead: range-only is rank { "2" } of { "4" } at a snapshot and needs an arc of about { "2.09" } h, range-rate (Doppler) gives rank { "4" } instantly, and co-estimating a station with satellite states and clocks stays rank-deficient by one without an external interferometric observable. Its numbers are regenerated by the <c1/> kind (<c2/>); <c3/> tests the threshold with an independent estimator. The README research card, the crates, npm and PyPI READMEs and the generator carry it.

