seg-ef81eca03bca = <c1/> gains six tools that drive the <c2/> layer from an agent: <c3/> (the accepted signal names and the IQ set-up), <c4/> (describe a recording), <c5/> (generate a stated-profile or broadcast-ephemeris scene, with the optional signal-level channel), <c6/> (FFT acquisition, optionally behind front-end stages), <c7/> (acquire, then the DLL/PLL/FLL bank) and <c8/> (band-pass, notch, blanking, excision, AGC, quantiser). IQ samples never cross the protocol: every tool takes file paths inside one work directory set by <c9/> (unset leaves the IQ file tools off and every other tool unaffected), refuses paths that resolve outside it and outputs that already exist unless <c10/> is set, enforces a per-call sample budget (<c11/>, default { "50 000 000" }) before any work, refuses unknown arguments, and replies with a compact JSON summary (detections, C/N0, lock state, and each file written with its byte count). Per-epoch output goes only to files the caller names. The tools run the same code path as the CLI through a new public seam, <c12/>, which returns the CLI's message instead of printing it (stdout is the MCP JSON-RPC channel); <c13/> and <c14/> are now public too. Software-only and additive: the IQ layer adds no interference or spoofer synthesis, nothing is ever transmitted, and there are no new dependencies. <c15/> declares the two environment variables. Round-trip tests in <c16/> generate a short two-satellite scene and check acquisition against the scene's own truth sidecar, tracking lock and C/N0, the front end, SigMF output, the budget, path confinement and the disabled state.

