seg-3c3dacae06d6 = <b><c1/>: how long a small drone's navigation holds after GNSS is jammed.</b> It runs a flight-controller-class micro-electro-mechanical (MEMS) inertial unit, a Bosch BMI088, against a tactical-grade unit. The GNSS (Global Navigation Satellite System) signal is nominal for { "100" } s, then denied for { "60" } s (<c2/> shows why a nearby jammer does that). The BMI088's biases are the residuals left after start-up calibration, taken from the temperature coefficients in its datasheet (BST-BMI088-DS000-19 rev { "1.9" }): { "0.015" } °/s per K for the gyro and < { "0.2" } mg per K for the accelerometer. Two assumptions are flagged in the file: a { "10" } K warm-up after calibration, and continued warming of { "1" } K per minute in flight. The in-flight drift is the error the filter cannot learn before the jamming starts, and it is what ends the coast. Every figure is modelled; validating it needs flight logs from a jammed environment. <c3/> prints it.

